# Configuration — copy to .env (start.bat / start.sh does this on first run).
#
# ALL SETTINGS ARE IN THE BLOCK BELOW. What each one does, and when you'd change it, is in
# the REFERENCE section further down (same order, same headings).
#
#   • Active lines are the ones you must set. `#KEY=value` lines are optional and show the
#     real default — uncomment only what you want to change.
#   • A line starting `#KEY=` is a setting; a line starting `# ` is prose.
#   • No inline comments after a value — the whole line after '=' is the value.


# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#                                  SETTINGS
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# --- Required (all filled in for you) ---
POSTGRES_USER=app
POSTGRES_DB=app
# The database password. start.bat/start.sh add a random one on a fresh install.
# POSTGRES_PASSWORD=(generated by start.bat / start.sh on first run)
# Encrypts RCON passwords at rest in the database. start.bat/start.sh generate one on first
# run automatically. KEEP IT SAFE: if it is lost, stored server passwords cannot be decrypted
# and each server's password must be re-entered.
# ENCRYPTION_KEY=(generated by start.bat / start.sh on first run)
# Signs logins. Leave it unset: the app makes its own on first boot and keeps it in the database.
#SESSION_SECRET=

# --- Ports ---
API_PORT=8080
POSTGRES_PORT=5432
REDIS_PORT=6379

# --- Bare-metal only (docker-compose sets these for you) ---
#DATABASE_URL=postgres://app:app@localhost:5432/app
#REDIS_URL=redis://localhost:6379
#WEB_ROOT=/app/apps/panel/out
#APP_VERSION=

# --- Putting the panel online: set in the panel (Administration → Online access) ---
# A VM install turns on the HTTPS proxy for its own domain with this (install.sh writes it).
#COMPOSE_PROFILES=domain

# --- Public stats site: set in the panel (Administration → Online access) ---

# --- Game servers (auto-discovery; every discovered server starts read-only) ---
#SERVER_1_HOST=1.2.3.4
#SERVER_1_PORT=28865
#SERVER_1_PASSWORD=changeme
#SERVER_1_GAME=hll
#SERVER_1_HEADER_MODE=magic
#SERVER_1_NAME=My Server
# A Hell Let Loose: Vietnam server — SERVER_n_GAME is what makes it read as Vietnam.
#SERVER_2_HOST=1.2.3.4
#SERVER_2_PORT=27230
#SERVER_2_PASSWORD=changeme
#SERVER_2_GAME=hllv

# --- Discord: the bot token, sign-in and the slash command are set in the panel (Discord → Setup & status) ---

# --- Discord: relay channels ---
#DISCORD_CHANNEL_ID=
#DISCORD_KILLS_CHANNEL_ID=
#DISCORD_ADMIN_CHANNEL_ID=
#DISCORD_MATCH_CHANNEL_ID=
#DISCORD_CHAT_CHANNEL_ID=
#DISCORD_CONNECTIONS_CHANNEL_ID=
#DISCORD_RELAY_CATEGORIES=kills,admin,match

# --- Backups ---
# Host folder that receives full backups and trim archives (mounted at /data/backups inside the
# api and worker containers). Default: a `backups` folder next to the repo — visible on this
# machine the moment a backup finishes. Point it anywhere (an external drive, a synced folder)
# to change where they land.
#BACKUPS_DIR=../backups

# --- CRCON backup / import ---
#CRCON_BACKUP_DIR=/data/crcon-backups
#CRCON_IMPORT_INTERVAL_MS=250
#CRCON_IMPORT_SCAN_MS=20000

# --- Logging ---
#LOG_LEVEL=info
#LOG_FORMAT=

# --- Tuning: log ingestion (ms) ---
#INGEST_POLL_MS=10000
#INGEST_RECONNECT_MS=15000
#INGEST_RESCAN_MS=60000

# --- Tuning: background runners (ms) ---
#SEED_POLL_MS=30000
#AUTOSETTINGS_POLL_MS=30000
#MAPVOTE_POLL_MS=30000
#BROADCAST_IDLE_MS=15000
#VIP_SWEEP_MS=120000
#AUTOSETTINGS_ENFORCE_MS=300000
#BAN_RECONCILE_MS=300000

# --- Tuning: Discord runners (ms) ---
#DISCORD_FLUSH_MS=2000
#DISCORD_RELAY_REFRESH_MS=20000
#DISCORD_WATCH_COOLDOWN_MS=300000
#DISCORD_ROLE_SYNC_MS=600000

# --- Debug ---
#INGEST_DEBUG=1

# --- Dev only: standalone observe script ---
#RCON_HOST=1.2.3.4
#RCON_PORT=28865
#RCON_PASSWORD=changeme
#RCON_HEADER_MODE=magic
#RCON_SERVER_ID=dev-server


# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#                                 REFERENCE
#              What each setting above does. Same order, same headings.
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# --- Required ---
# POSTGRES_USER / POSTGRES_PASSWORD / POSTGRES_DB
#   docker-compose builds DATABASE_URL from these automatically. The start script writes a random
#   POSTGRES_PASSWORD when it creates this file for a brand-new install. An install whose database
#   already exists keeps the password it was made with (`app` on older installs) — changing it here
#   does not change the database's, and the app could no longer connect.
# SESSION_SECRET
#   Signs login sessions. Leave it unset: the app makes a random key on first boot and keeps it in
#   the database, so logins survive restarts. Set it only to pin a key of your own. The placeholder
#   older copies of this file carried is ignored — it was public — and replaced the same way.

# --- Ports ---
# API_PORT       Host port the panel + API are served on. When something else holds it, the start
#                scripts offer another and save it here; open http://localhost:<that port>. Inside
#                Docker the API listens on 8080 either way. Run without Docker, `PORT` is accepted as an alias for hosts that inject it;
#                API_PORT wins if both are set.
# POSTGRES_PORT  Host port for Postgres — change only if it clashes with something local. Open on
#                this machine only (127.0.0.1): nothing on the network can reach the database.
# REDIS_PORT     Host port for Redis, also this machine only. Redis carries live events from the
#                worker to the API's plugins (kill feed, alerts, …).

# --- Bare-metal only ---
# DATABASE_URL / REDIS_URL
#   Under docker-compose these are composed for you and must NOT be set here. Set them only
#   for a non-compose install.
# WEB_ROOT
#   Directory of the built panel that the API serves. Compose sets this; leave unset to run
#   API-only with no web UI.
# APP_VERSION
#   Optional display label shown in the sidebar. The RUNNING COMMIT is stamped automatically:
#   start.bat / start.sh pass it into the image build, and it shows in the sidebar and on
#   Administration → System — that stamp, not this label, is what tells you an update landed.

# --- Putting the panel online ---
#   Both ways are set in the panel, Administration → Online access, with their pros and cons and
#   the steps for each:
#     - a Cloudflare tunnel, which the panel runs itself from the token pasted there (the way for a
#       home PC, and fine on a VM), or
#     - your own domain with HTTPS, on a VM: the HTTPS proxy (infra/Caddyfile) fetches the
#       certificate from Let's Encrypt.
# COMPOSE_PROFILES=domain
#   Runs that HTTPS proxy, on ports 80 and 443. The VM installer sets it; a home PC leaves it off.
# CLOUDFLARE_TUNNEL_TOKEN
#   How older installs ran the tunnel: a separate container started with this token. The panel
#   carried it over on its first start with this version and runs the tunnel itself from then on;
#   start.bat / start.sh remove the old container once the panel's own tunnel is connected.

# --- Public stats site ---
#   Its own address, the address its links start with, and the community name it wears are set in
#   the panel: Administration → Online access. An older copy of this file that set
#   PUBLIC_STATS_HOST, PUBLIC_STATS_ORIGIN or PUBLIC_STATS_NAME had them carried over there on its
#   first start with this version; from then on the panel's values are the ones in force.

# --- Plugins ---
#   Nothing to set here: plugins go in the installed-plugins folder (see its README.md), which the
#   panel always reads.

# --- Game servers (auto-discovery) ---
#   You can add servers in the panel (Config → Connections), OR seed them here and they're
#   auto-discovered on startup — handy for compose / hosted deploys. Discovery is idempotent
#   (keyed on host:port), so it never duplicates a server you already added.
#
#   SAFETY: every discovered server starts in `observe` mode (read-only). It cannot issue a
#   single write command until you switch it to active in the panel, so this is safe to run
#   alongside an existing CRCON on the same game server.
#
#   The display NAME is pulled live over RCON, so SERVER_n_NAME is only a fallback until that
#   succeeds. Indices need not be contiguous — every SERVER_<n>_HOST present is discovered.
#   SERVER_n_HEADER_MODE is `magic` (current servers) or `legacy` (older ones).
#
#   SERVER_n_GAME says WHICH GAME the server runs: `hll` (the default, so existing setups need
#   no change) or `hllv` for Hell Let Loose: Vietnam. Set it — Vietnam speaks HLL's RCON wire for
#   wire, so a Vietnam server left as `hll` connects perfectly and is then read with the wrong
#   roles, factions, weapons and player-identity rule, with nothing to warn you. A game this
#   build has no module for is refused outright at startup (see the log) rather than defaulted.
#   The game can also be corrected later under Administration → Connections.
#
#   SERVER_n_PROTOCOL defaults to the game's current protocol and rarely needs setting. The one
#   case that does: `crcon-bridge` (HLL only), which reads through an existing CRCON instead of
#   connecting directly, and needs SERVER_n_CRCON_URL plus that instance's API key as the
#   PASSWORD.

# --- Discord (all of it optional) ---
#   The Discord app's settings — the bot token, sign-in with Discord and the slash command — are set
#   in the panel: Discord → Setup & status, which also shows which features each one unlocks. An
#   older copy of this file that set DISCORD_TOKEN, DISCORD_CLIENT_ID, DISCORD_CLIENT_SECRET,
#   DISCORD_GUILD_ID, DISCORD_OAUTH_REDIRECT, DISCORD_PUBLIC_KEY or DISCORD_APP_ID had them carried
#   over there on its first start with this version; from then on the panel's values are in force.
#
# DISCORD_CHANNEL_ID and DISCORD_<CATEGORY>_CHANNEL_ID
#   Where live game activity posts. Per-server routing is configurable in the panel
#   (Discord → Relay & alerts); these are the fallback for servers with no panel config yet.
#   Each per-category id falls back to DISCORD_CHANNEL_ID when unset.
# DISCORD_RELAY_CATEGORIES
#   Which categories relay by default — comma list of: kills,chat,admin,connections,match.
#   chat and connections are opt-in: they're noisy and more privacy-sensitive.

# --- CRCON backup / import ---
#   Pulls an existing CRCON instance's stored data into a per-server archive, then transforms
#   it into the panel (Config → Connections → CRCON backup). The archive is one NDJSON file per
#   server (never the DB), streamed so memory and Postgres stay small even on huge pulls.
#   Multi-server CRCON: each game server has its own CRCON URL + API key. Add each as a
#   panel server with that URL/key and run a pull on each — the shared player DB de-dupes.
#
# CRCON_BACKUP_DIR
#   Where archive files live. Compose sets /data/crcon-backups on a shared volume for you; set
#   this only for bare-metal. It MUST be a persistent path shared by api + worker — if it's a
#   temp dir, a reboot mid-pull loses the file and you'd re-pull from scratch. Default when
#   unset: the OS temp dir.
# CRCON_IMPORT_INTERVAL_MS / CRCON_IMPORT_SCAN_MS
#   Pace of the pull. Gentle by default so it never bursts against CRCON. Raise the interval to
#   go slower; lower it (min 0) to pull faster. SCAN is how often the worker looks for a
#   new/resumed pull to drive.

# --- Logging ---
# LOG_LEVEL   debug | info | warn | error
# LOG_FORMAT  set to `json` for one JSON object per line (for log aggregators)

# --- Tuning ---
#   Defaults are sane — you generally only touch these to reduce load on a small host or to
#   react faster on a big one. All values are milliseconds.
#
# INGEST_POLL_MS        how often each server's admin log is read
# INGEST_RECONNECT_MS   wait before reconnecting after a dropped connection
# INGEST_RESCAN_MS      how often the worker looks for newly added servers
#
#   Each background runner opens its own slow-poll connection per server:
# SEED_POLL_MS          seeding rules + seed-VIP rewards (the default — each server can set its
#                       own on the Seeding tab → Check speed, which wins and needs no restart)
# AUTOSETTINGS_POLL_MS  scheduled server settings + player rules
# MAPVOTE_POLL_MS       map-vote poll lifecycle
# BROADCAST_IDLE_MS     rotating broadcast tick
# VIP_SWEEP_MS          expiry sweep for VIPs we granted
# AUTOSETTINGS_ENFORCE_MS  how often an active profile's settings are checked against the server
#                        and re-applied if drifted (default 300000 = 5 min). When drift proves a
#                        wipe, write-only settings (VIP slots, welcome message) are re-taught in
#                        the same pass. Separate from AUTOSETTINGS_POLL_MS on purpose: that one
#                        also paces the player rules.
# BAN_RECONCILE_MS      how often BOTH reconcilers below compare the panel against the game

# --- Reconcilers ---
#   Two background checks that compare what the PANEL decided against what each game server is
#   actually enforcing, and report the difference. They exist because the failure they catch is
#   silent: a game server accepts a command, applies it to nothing, and answers 200 — so a ban
#   that never landed, or a VIP list the server wiped, looks perfect in the panel forever.
#
#   Both run automatically on every ACTIVE server (observe servers are skipped entirely — even a
#   report would describe writes they can never make), and both can be switched off per server
#   under Administration → Connections → automation ("Ban reconciliation" / "VIP reconciliation").
#
#   Out of the box they only REPORT: findings land in the audit trail (Activity), attributed to
#   `ban-sync` / `vip-sync`, once per standing situation rather than once per tick. Whether one
#   also FIXES what it reports is set in the app, per server: the "Fix what it finds" switch
#   under each reconciler on that same automation list (off by default). There is no env flag
#   for it any more — BAN_RECONCILE_APPLY / VIP_RECONCILE_APPLY are ignored; an install that
#   armed them must switch the fixes on in the app.
#
#   Read a few days of reports before arming. Not because applying is dangerous — the plans are
#   conservative, and bans/VIPs placed outside the panel are always reported and never removed —
#   but because the reports tell you whether your servers agree with the panel in the first
#   place. Arming on top of a disagreement you have not understood is how a surprise becomes a
#   mass ban or a mass VIP grant. The Bans and VIPs pages each have a "Sync with game" button
#   that applies the same plan once for one server, with the plan shown first.
#
# DISCORD_FLUSH_MS            batching window before posting (avoids rate limits)
# DISCORD_RELAY_REFRESH_MS    how often per-server relay config is re-read
# DISCORD_WATCH_COOLDOWN_MS   min gap between watchlist alerts for the same player
# DISCORD_ROLE_SYNC_MS        re-sync Discord-managed users' roles, so removing someone's
#                             Discord role revokes their access without a re-login

# --- Debug ---
# INGEST_DEBUG
#   Set to 1 to make the worker dump the raw GetAdminLog response + extracted log lines once
#   per connection (docker compose logs worker). Use this to tune admin-log parsing against a
#   real server, then turn it back off — it's verbose.

# --- Dev only ---
# RCON_HOST / RCON_PORT / RCON_PASSWORD / RCON_HEADER_MODE / RCON_SERVER_ID
#   The read-only connection test (observe.bat / observe.mjs) reads its target from these,
#   separately from the SERVER_n_* discovery above. Nothing else uses them.
